BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4222a3034e71f88b@role-bankruptcy.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260919T050027Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260623
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Pung v. Isabella County
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 609 U. S. Part 1 \nPages 30–58 \n
 OFFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nJune 23\, 2026 \nREBECCA A. WO
 MELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTICE: 
 This preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound vo
 lume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Decisio
 ns\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \npio
 @supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \n30 OCTO
 BER TERM\, 2025 \nSyllabus \nPUNG\, PERSONAL REPRESENTATIVE OF THE \nESTAT
 E OF PUNG v. ISABELLA COUNTY\, \nMICHIGAN \ncertiorari to the united state
 s court of appeals for \nthe sixth circuit \nNo. 25–95. Argued February 
 25\, 2026—Decided June 23\, 2026 \nThe Pung family owed $2\,241.93 in re
 al-property taxes\, so local tax author-\nities in Isabella County\, Michi
 gan\, initiated foreclosure proceedings and \nsold the Pung home—which w
 as assessed at $194\,400 for tax purposes— \nfor $76\,008 at public auct
 ion. Michael Pung sued in Federal court\, and \nthe District Court granted
  Pung partial summary judgment on his Fifth \nAmendment claim. The court h
 eld that Pung should receive only the \nsurplus proceeds from the tax sale
 —i. e.\, the difference between the \nsale price and the tax debt—not 
 the property's fair market value. The \nDistrict Court also rejected Pung'
 s claim under the Eighth Amendment \nExcessive Fines Clause. The Sixth Cir
 cuit affrmed. \nHeld: \n1. The proper baseline for measuring “just compe
 nsation” following a \ntax sale is the auction sale price\, not the prop
 erty's hypothetical fair \nmarket value\, at least when the sale is fairly
  conducted in light of the \ncountry's history of tax sales. Pp. 35–43. 
 \n(a) For hundreds of years\, English and American law have allowed \nthe 
 seizure and sale of property as a tax-collection method\, provided \nthat 
 the government return any surplus proceeds to the debtor. Fed-\neral statu
 tes from the early days of the Republic applied this rule\, \nas did this 
 Court's precedents. Unite\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609
 us1r54_d18e.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r54_d18e.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:56b5452c40a3de77@role-bankruptcy.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260918T205310Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine's Minimum Wage to Increase to $15.70 Pe
 r Hour in 2027
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Maine's Mini
 mum Wage to Increase to $15.70 Per Hour in 2027\nServices\nJobs & Training
 \nUnemployment Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Law
 s\nLabor Statistics\nWorkforce Development\nMaine's Minimum Wage to Increa
 se to $15.70 Per Hour in 2027\nSeptember 18\, 2026\nFor Immediate Release:
  September 18\, 2026\nMedia Contact: Autumn Mahoney\, Communications Speci
 alist\nmedia.dol@maine.gov\nAUGUSTA - The Maine Department of Labor announ
 ced today that\, effective\nJanuary 1\, 2027\n\, the state minimum wage wi
 ll increase from $15.10 to\n$15.70\nper hour\, based on data recently made
  available by the U.S. Department of Labor's Bureau of Labor Statistics. M
 DOL is required under Maine law (Maine statute Title 26\, chapter 7\, sect
 ion 664) to make annual adjustments to the minimum wage based on the cost-
 of-living index (CPI-W) for the Northeast Region\, after Maine voters amen
 ded the law by referendum in 2016. Between August 2025 and August 2026\, t
 here was a\n4.1\npercent increase in the CPI-W.\nAccording to the U.S. Dep
 artment of Labor's Bureau of Labor Statistics\nCurrent Population Survey f
 or  2025\n\, there were 28\,000 hourly workers who were paid less than $15
 .00 per hour. These workers represent about four percent of all wage and s
 alary workers (both paid at hourly rates and not paid at hourly rates) and
  about eight percent of wage and salary workers who are paid at hourly rat
 es.\nAgricultural workers in Maine are also entitled to the state minimum 
 wage\, after the Legislature enacted and Governor Mills signed "\nAn Act t
 o Establish a State Minimum Hourly Wage for Agricultural Workers\n" in 202
 5.\nThe "tip wage\," or service employee minimum wage\, will increase from
  $7.55 to\n$7.85 p\nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?i
 d=13362373
URL:http://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13362373
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
